  "schema:description": "The inter leaflet transport of phospholipids is a crucial physiological process that takes place in the cell plasma membrane. This process can be facilitated by specific proteins – scramblases – without the consumption of ATP. Experimental data, provided here, describe the lipid scrambling activity of the Sec61/TRAP translocon. Sec61/TRAP has previously been described as a protein complex that maintainins the membrane transition of the nascent amino acid chain. In our work, we reconstituted Sec61/TRAP into large unilamellar vesicles and used experiments based on NBD fluorescence quenching by sodium dithionite or bovine serum albumin to investigate the scrambling activity of Sec61/TRAP. Both complementary approaches demonstrated lipid scrambling. Blocking the Sec61 translocation cavity did not affect scrambling, supporting the existence of an alternative scrambling pathway in the translocon complex.\n\nMethods: Sec61/TRAP was reconstituted in Large Unilamellar Vesicles, containing 0.4 % (mol) of NBD labelled lipid. The labelled lipid is supposed to be distributed equally in both membrane leaflets. Once the Sodium Dithionite is added to experimental mixture, it quenches all the sterically available NBD molecules. Thus, observed fluorescence decrease to ~ 50 % of initial intensity. However, if there is present a scramblase, which maintains fast translocation of phospholipids from one to the other leaflet, the NBD fluorescence decrease to 0, in an ideal case. Because Sodium Dithionite is a small molecule which can theoretically penetrate the membrane via any pores, we replicated the experiments also with bovine serum albumin. This protein can quench 50 % of NBD fluorescence, and due to its size, it cannot penetrate the membrane. Experiments were performed in presence of different inhibitors, showing that sterical block of Sec61 cavity does not influence the lipid scrambling – such a result support the relevance of TRAP scrambling activity. Large Unilamellar Vesicles (LUVs), composed of 99.6% (mol) POPC and 0.4% (mol) of 16:0- 12:0 NBD PS; or 0.4% (mol) of 16:0-06:0 NBD PC (Avanti), were prepared by extrusion. Chloroform solutions of POPC and NBD labeled PC or PS were mixed in a round bottom test tube and dried under a mild flow of nitrogen for 10 minutes. After that, dry lipids were incubated under vacuum overnight. Next day, lipids were hydrated in 500 μL of SECb buffer (50 mM Hepes, 300 mM KOAc, 10 mM MgCl2, 1 mM DTT, pH 7.5) for 30 min at room temperature and vortexed for 1 min. Multilamellar liposomes, present in the solution after vortexing, were extruded through a 200 nm membrane. For the protein reconstitution in the membrane, we followed a procedure introduced by Brunner and Schenck. Specifically, 75 μL of LUVs were mixed with 37 μL of the protein, 15 μL of Triton X-100 (10 % (v/w)), and 248 μL of the SECb buffer. Thus, the final total lipid concentration was 800 μM, and the final protein concentration was 109.5 nM. This mixture was incubated at room temperature for one hour with a gentle mixing. Then, 100 mg of Bio-Beads SM2 (BioRad) was added, and the mixture was incubated overnight and gently mixed at 277 K. In parallel, the negative control was prepared in the same way, containing 37 μL of the SECb buffer instead of protein solution. The size distribution of LUVs, containing reconstituted translocon, was controlled using DLS (Zetasizer Nano S, Malvern Panalytical). The dithionite assay was performed at 96 well plate (Nunc MicroWell 96-Well Optical- Bottom, Thermo Scientific) using Infinite M Nano+ (Tecan) plate reader. Samples containing the reconstituted proteins and negative controls were measured in the same run. For the experiment, 17 μL of the reconstitution mixture was added to 183 μL of SECb buffer. Light with a wavelength of 460 nm was used for the excitation, whereas the emission was detected at 535 nm. After temperature equilibration, the fluorescence intensity was measured in 20 s intervals for at least 180 s to acquire the initial plateau. Then, 30 μL of 0.3 M sodium dithionate was added to all samples including the negative controls. The fluorescence decay curves (F (t)) were normalized so that their maximum intensity was equal to 1. The same setup of the plate reader and the same composition of the initial reaction mixtures, as described for the dithionite assay, was used for the BSA assay. After temperature equilibration and acquirement of an initial plateau, 30 μL of fatty acid-free BSA (25 mg/mL) was added. The fluorescence curves were fitted with Eq. (4), as for the dithionite assay.\n\nTechnical information: Infinite M Nano+ plate reader writes measured values to a MS Excel sheet. Here are available Excel files from 4 experimental series, which differ in membrane compositions and inhibitor presence. Every file contains sheets, according to experiments with different quenching approach or inhibitor presence. \"dit\" means Sodium Dithionite quenching, BSA means quenching with BSA, A is for Apratoxin A presence and I is for Ipomoeasin F inhibiton. PC,PC,A.xlsx – LUVs composed of 99.6% (mol) POPC, 0.4% (mol) of 16:0-06:0 NBD PC, Apratoxin A inhibition PC,PC,I.xlsx – LUVs composed of 99.6% (mol) POPC, 0.4% (mol) of 16:0-06:0 NBD PC, Ipomoeassin F inhibiton PC,PS,A.xlsx – LUVs composed of 99.6% (mol) POPC and 0.4% (mol) of 16:0- 12:0 NBD PS, Apratoxin A inhibition PC,PS,I.xslx – composed of 99.6% (mol) POPC and 0.4% (mol) of 16:0- 12:0 NBD PS, Ipomoeassin F inhibiton Produced sheet contains heading with all parameters of the measurement – excitation / emission wavelength, gain, interval time, etc. Raw data are available in a table below the parameters of measurement. In this table, first column is for number of cycle, second for time, third for temperature and following columns represent fluorescence intensity of particular wells. This data was normalized and averaged – green table in every sheet. Also, the time gap for pipetting during the experiment was subtracted to keep the experimental time continuous. Final version of processed data for each experiment is available at sheet \"Summary\".",
